---
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";

/**
 * The Activity window, which was missing from this site entirely — the one
 * claim on the page that no competing server even attempts, and it was the one
 * the reader never saw. A server spawned inside an editor is unobservable by
 * construction: there is no process to look at and no window to open. This
 * section exists to make that difference concrete.
 *
 * Drawn as real window chrome rather than illustrated, for the same reason
 * MenuBar is: a reader who has used the app recognises it, and a mock that
 * matches the product needs no caption. It is also why the hero's card is NOT
 * dressed as a window — that one diagrams a turn the app never renders, and
 * chrome there would promise a pane that does not exist.
 *
 * THE ROWS DELIBERATELY CARRY NO ARGUMENTS. That is not a drawing decision, it
 * is what the app records — tool names only, never arguments, message contents
 * or results. A mock showing a subject line here would advertise a feature the
 * app does not have and would not want.
 */
const rows = [
  { t: "09:41:02", client: "Claude Desktop", tool: "apple_mail_search_messages", kind: "read" },
  { t: "09:41:02", client: "Claude Desktop", tool: "apple_mail_get_thread", kind: "read" },
  { t: "09:41:03", client: "Claude Desktop", tool: "apple_notes_search_notes", kind: "read" },
  { t: "09:40:55", client: "Cursor", tool: "apple_reminders_list_reminders", kind: "read" },
  { t: "09:40:12", client: "Cursor", tool: "apple_mail_list_accounts", kind: "read" },
] as const;
---

<section id="activity" class="wrap scroll-mt-20 py-[96px]">
  <SectionHeader eyebrow="Activity" heading="You can watch it<br />the whole time.">
    Every tool call, live, grouped by the client that made it. It is the answer to “what did the
    assistant just do with my mail?” — and the reason the servers run under an app you can see
    rather than inside whichever editor happened to spawn them.
  </SectionHeader>

  <div
    class="reveal overflow-hidden rounded-[14px] border border-rule-2 bg-chrome shadow-[0_24px_60px_-34px_rgba(0,0,0,.9)]"
  >
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-rule-2 bg-chrome-2 px-3.5 py-2.5">
      <span class="flex gap-[6px]" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="block rounded-full bg-fg-muted/40" style="width:10px;height:10px"></span>
        <span class="block rounded-full bg-fg-muted/40" style="width:10px;height:10px"></span>
        <span class="block rounded-full bg-fg-muted/40" style="width:10px;height:10px"></span>
      </span>
      <span class="mx-auto text-[12px] text-fg">Activity</span>
    </div>

    {/* Scrolls inside itself: five columns of monospace do not fit a phone, and
        a table that widens the page is worse than one that scrolls. */}
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <ul class="m-0 min-w-[440px] list-none p-0">
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <li class="flex items-center gap-3 border-b border-[color-mix(in_srgb,var(--color-rule)_70%,transparent)] px-3.5 py-[9px] last:border-b-0">
              <span class="mono flex-none">{r.t}</span>
              <span class="flex-none text-[12.5px] text-fg-dim">{r.client}</span>
              <span class="flex-1 truncate font-mono text-[12px] text-accent">{r.tool}</span>
              <span class="mono flex-none !text-ok">{r.kind}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mono border-t border-rule-2 bg-chrome-2 px-3.5 py-2.5">
      tool names only · no arguments, no contents, no results
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal mt-6 text-[14px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-muted">
    The menu bar shows the same thing one level up: which client is talking to which server right
    now, and how many tools it has called. None of it is sent anywhere. The one connection Cupertino
    can make is the update check, it is off until you turn it on, and it carries no identifier —
    which CI checks against the built binary rather than the source.
  </p>
</section>
